<section class="page-header card">
  <p class="eyebrow">About</p>
  <h2>Deadline for Unfinished Essays</h2>
  <p class="lede">DUE is a writing experiment: every essay ships within 30 days—finished or not. Public participation happens through comments on published essays; drafting and edits occur in the backend so schedules stay predictable.</p>
</section>

<section class="card-grid">
  <article class="card panel">
    <p class="eyebrow">Cadence</p>
    <h3 class="card-title">30 days to publish</h3>
    <p class="muted">Once an essay begins, the deadline is set. On that date it goes live in whatever shape it is in, then iterates through new releases.</p>
  </article>
  <article class="card panel">
    <p class="eyebrow">Credit</p>
    <h3 class="card-title">Acknowledgment culture</h3>
    <p class="muted">Minor contributions are credited in acknowledgments. Major contributions earn coauthorship. Release notes and version history capture the journey.</p>
  </article>
  <article class="card panel">
    <p class="eyebrow">Length</p>
    <h3 class="card-title">Color-coded badges</h3>
    <p class="muted">Purple squares flag Tiny pieces (100–500 words), orange triangles cover Minute essays (500–1000), and teal circles highlight Short dives up to 1500 words.</p>
  </article>
</section>

